      "Primary Mechanism: Radial head subluxation, also known as 'Nursemaid's elbow,' occurs when sudden longitudinal traction is applied to an extended and pronated arm. This force causes the annular ligament to slip over the head of the radius and become trapped within the radiohumeral joint space.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The classic presentation involves a young child (typically 1-4 years old) who refuses to use an arm after being pulled or lifted by the hand. The characteristic physical exam finding is an arm held in a pronated, slightly flexed position close to the body. The lack of swelling, bruising, or deformity helps distinguish this from more severe fractures.",
      "Key Association: This condition is synonymous with 'Nursemaid's elbow.' Treatment involves reduction via gentle supination of the forearm with the elbow flexed, or alternatively, hyperpronation."
