      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient experienced numbness (a sensory deficit) in her left leg. Sensory information is processed in the primary somatosensory cortex, located in the postcentral gyrus, which is posterior to the central sulcus. Because the symptoms occurred on the left side of the body, the lesion must be in the right hemisphere (contralateral). Therefore, the combination of 'left side' (right hemisphere), 'leg' (medial aspect), and 'numbness' (posterior to the central sulcus) points to the right medial hemisphere, posterior to the central sulcus.",
