    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": {
      "system": "Hematology",
      "discipline": "Pathology"
    },
    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Hereditary spherocytosis (HS) is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by mutations in genes encoding proteins involved in the red blood cell (RBC) membrane-cytoskeleton anchor, such as ankyrin-1 (most common), spectrin, or band 3. These defects lead to a progressive loss of the RBC membrane as it passes through the splenic sinusoids, resulting in a decreased surface-area-to-volume ratio and the formation of spherocytes. These spherical cells are poorly deformable and are sequestered and destroyed by splenic macrophages (extravascular hemolysis).",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's father's history of splenectomy and cholecystectomy is a classic clue for HS. Splenectomy reduces the rate of hemolysis by removing the site of RBC destruction, while cholecystectomy is often necessary due to the formation of calcium bilirubinate (pigment) stones from chronic hyperbilirubinemia. HS is typically a normocytic anemia, meaning the Mean Corpuscular Volume (MCV) remains within the normal range of 80-100 fL. The peripheral smear description of robust cells lacking central pallor confirms the presence of spherocytes.",
      "Key Association: A family history of splenectomy and cholecystectomy in the setting of spherocytes on a peripheral smear is a high-yield indicator for Hereditary Spherocytosis."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Incorrect because chronic hemolysis leads to an increase in unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin. Direct bilirubin (conjugated) would only be elevated in cases of biliary obstruction or certain liver diseases.",
      "Option B: Incorrect because Hereditary Spherocytosis is typically an autosomal dominant condition, meaning it results from a heterozygous mutation. Homozygous mutations in these proteins are often lethal or result in much more severe phenotypes.",
      "Option D: Incorrect because a positive direct antiglobulin (Coombs) test indicates an immune-mediated hemolysis (e.g., Autoimmune Hemolytic Anemia). HS is a structural membrane defect, so the Coombs test is negative.",
      "Option E: Incorrect because MCHC is actually increased in HS (often >36 g/dL) due to mild cellular dehydration and the loss of membrane surface area relative to the hemoglobin content."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Hereditary spherocytosis is a normocytic, normochromic anemia characterized by a negative Coombs test, increased MCHC, and spherocytes on peripheral smear, often presenting with a family history of splenectomy and pigment gallstones.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Q #1470 \u2013 Hematology",
    "id": "7f2828a9"
