      "Primary Mechanism: Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is a type III hypersensitivity reaction occurring 1-3 weeks after a Group A Streptococcus (GAS) infection (pharyngitis or impetigo). It involves the deposition of immune complexes in the glomerular basement membrane. Two key antigens involved are nephritis-associated plasmin receptor (NAPlr) and streptococcal pyrogenic exotoxin B (SpeB). These antigens trigger antibody formation, leading to immune complex deposition, complement activation (lowering C3 levels), and subsequent glomerular inflammation.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's presentation of cola-colored urine (hematuria) and periorbital edema following a recent sore throat is classic for PSGN. The latent period of 2 weeks is characteristic, distinguishing it from IgA nephropathy, which typically occurs within days of an upper respiratory infection.",
