      "Primary Mechanism: Exogenous glucocorticoids, such as the prednisone likely used to manage this patient's SLE, exert negative feedback on the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. This feedback inhibits the release of Corticotropin-Releasing Hormone (CRH) from the hypothalamus.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: Under normal physiological conditions, CRH stimulates the production of pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) in the anterior pituitary. POMC is a large precursor protein that is cleaved into several active peptides, including Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH) and Melanocyte-Stimulating Hormone (MSH). When CRH is suppressed by exogenous steroids, the synthesis of its downstream precursor, POMC, significantly decreases.",
