      "Option C: Anaplasmosis is also transmitted by the Ixodes tick but is a bacterial infection (Anaplasma phagocytophilum). It typically presents with flu-like symptoms and is characterized by morulae (berry-like clusters) within granulocytes (neutrophils), not ring forms in RBCs.",
      "Option D: Malaria (Plasmodium species) can present with identical ring forms and hemolytic symptoms. However, it is almost exclusively seen in patients with recent travel to endemic regions (e.g., Africa, South America). Furthermore, Plasmodium does not form the characteristic Maltese cross tetrad."
