      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient presents with the classic 'Centor criteria' (fever, exudates, lymphadenopathy, absence of cough), which often mimics Group A Streptococcal pharyngitis. Because the rapid strep test has low sensitivity, clinicians often start empiric amoxicillin while waiting for culture results. In the setting of EBV, this drug-virus interaction triggers an immune-mediated rash that is not a true IgE-mediated allergy.",
