      "Primary Mechanism: Flucloxacillin belongs to the methicillin class of beta-lactams (alongside dicloxacillin, nafcillin, and oxacillin). These drugs possess bulky functional groups that provide steric hindrance, making them resistant to the beta-lactamase (penicillinase) enzymes produced by most strains of Staphylococcus aureus.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's presentation of erythema and desquamation is consistent with a localized skin infection such as cellulitis or impetigo. The primary pathogens to cover are Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes. Because approximately 90% of community-acquired S. aureus (MSSA) produce beta-lactamase, simple penicillins like amoxicillin are ineffective. Flucloxacillin provides excellent coverage for both MSSA and Group A Strep.",
