    "question": "A 55-year-old male presents with a 3-hour history of confusion. Physical examination is otherwise unremarkable, specifically noting the absence of neck stiffness. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) analysis reveals normal glucose and protein levels, but shows an elevated lymphocyte count and the presence of red blood cells (RBCs). An EEG is performed and demonstrates periodic spikes over the temporal regions. Which of the following best describes the most likely causative agent?",
