    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Mobitz II is a type of second-degree heart block typically caused by a block in the His-Purkinje system. Unlike Mobitz I (Wenckebach), which involves the AV node and shows progressive PR prolongation, Mobitz II maintains a constant PR interval before a beat is dropped.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: Mobitz II is considered dangerous because it frequently progresses to third-degree (complete) heart block, which can lead to fatal arrhythmias or sudden cardiac death. Because of this instability, a pacemaker is indicated regardless of symptoms.",
      "Key Association: Mobitz II is characterized by a 'dropped' QRS complex without any prior PR interval lengthening, distinguishing it from the 'longer, longer, longer, drop' pattern of Mobitz I."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Observation is appropriate for first-degree heart block and Mobitz I (Wenckebach) in asymptomatic patients, but Mobitz II requires intervention due to the risk of progression.",
      "Option B: Atropine can be used for acute symptomatic bradycardia but does not address the underlying conduction defect in Mobitz II and is not a long-term solution.",
      "Option C: Procainamide is a class IA antiarrhythmic used in the management of Wolff-Parkinson-White (WPW) syndrome, not for heart block."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Mobitz II second-degree heart block is characterized by a constant PR interval with dropped QRS complexes and requires a permanent pacemaker due to the high risk of progression to complete heart block.",
