    "question": "A 58-year-old male smoker presents with flank pain and a palpable abdominal mass. Laboratory studies reveal a serum calcium of 10.8 mg/dL (normal: 8.4\u201310.2 mg/dL) and a hemoglobin of 18 g/dL. A biopsy of the renal mass is performed, and histology shows large cells with clear cytoplasm. Which of the following is most likely to be found in this patient?",
    "vitals": null,
    "options": [
      "A: Decreased erythropoietin (EPO)",
      "B: Elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH)",
      "C: Hematuria",
      "D: Hemoptysis"
    ],
    "correct_answer": "C: Hematuria",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": {
      "system": "Renal",
      "discipline": "Pathology"
    },
    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC), specifically the clear cell variant, is the most common primary renal malignancy. It is characterized histologically by cells with abundant clear cytoplasm (due to high glycogen and lipid content) and a prominent vascular network. RCC is notorious for producing paraneoplastic syndromes, most notably the secretion of erythropoietin (EPO), which causes secondary polycythemia (elevated hemoglobin), and parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHrP), which causes hypercalcemia.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's presentation of flank pain, elevated hemoglobin (18 g/dL), and hypercalcemia (10.8 mg/dL) strongly suggests RCC. Hematuria is the most common clinical finding in RCC, occurring in approximately 60% of patients, and is part of the classic (though rarely complete) triad of hematuria, flank pain, and a palpable mass.",
      "Key Association: Remember the 'PEAR' mnemonic for RCC paraneoplastic syndromes: PTHrP, Epo, ACTH, and Renin. The presence of 'large clear cells' on histology is the high-yield buzzword for this diagnosis."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Incorrect because RCC typically secretes EPO autonomously, leading to elevated levels and secondary polycythemia, rather than decreased levels.",
      "Option B: Incorrect because the hypercalcemia in RCC is driven by PTHrP, which mimics PTH at the receptor but actually suppresses endogenous PTH production through negative feedback at the parathyroid glands.",
      "Option D: Incorrect because hemoptysis is a classic sign of lung cancer or pulmonary hemorrhage. While RCC can metastasize to the lungs, hematuria is a more direct and common finding for the primary renal tumor."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) typically presents in smokers with hematuria, flank pain, and paraneoplastic syndromes such as polycythemia (via EPO) and hypercalcemia (via PTHrP); histology classically shows large clear cells.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Q #1360 \u2013 Pathology",
    "id": "802a57e5"
