    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: In Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA), hyperkalemia (elevated serum potassium) occurs primarily through two mechanisms: cellular shift and insulin deficiency. As ketoacids accumulate, the resulting metabolic acidosis causes hydrogen ions (H+) to move into cells; to maintain electroneutrality, potassium ions (K+) shift out of the cells into the extracellular fluid. Additionally, insulin normally stimulates the Na+/K+ ATPase pump to drive potassium into cells; its absence in DKA prevents this uptake.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: Despite the high serum potassium levels, the patient actually has a 'low total body potassium.' This is because the excess potassium in the blood is filtered by the kidneys and excreted in the urine (kaliuresis), often exacerbated by osmotic diuresis from hyperglycemia. The low bicarbonate reflects the consumption of buffer by ketoacids (metabolic acidosis), and the low pCO2 is the result of respiratory compensation (Kussmaul breathing) to blow off CO2 and raise the pH.",
      "Key Association: Remember the 'paradoxical hyperkalemia' of DKA\u2014the blood says high, but the body is actually depleted."
    ],
