    "question": "An 8-month-old male infant is brought to the pediatrician for a routine check-up. The mother reports that the infant has been exclusively breastfed since birth and has not yet started solid foods. On physical examination, the physician notes a widening of the wrists and a slight bowing of the legs. Radiographic imaging of the wrist reveals splaying and fraying of the distal metaphyses. Which of the following laboratory findings is most likely to be increased in this patient's serum?",
    "vitals": null,
    "options": [
      "A: 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D",
      "B: Alkaline phosphatase activity",
      "C: Calcium",
      "D: Phosphate",
      "E: Unconjugated bilirubin"
    ],
    "correct_answer": "B: Alkaline phosphatase activity",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": {
      "system": "Musculoskeletal",
      "discipline": "Pathology"
    },
    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Vitamin D is essential for the absorption of calcium and phosphate in the small intestine. In nutritional rickets (often due to exclusive breastfeeding without supplementation), low vitamin D leads to decreased serum calcium. This hypocalcemia triggers the parathyroid glands to increase the secretion of Parathyroid Hormone (PTH). PTH binds to receptors on osteoblasts, stimulating their activity. Increased osteoblast activity is reflected by an elevation in serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP).",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient presents with classic signs of rickets: widening of the wrists and metaphyseal splaying/fraying on X-ray. These findings result from a failure to mineralize the osteoid matrix at the growth plates. While PTH attempts to normalize calcium levels, the increased bone turnover and osteoblast compensation lead to high ALP.",
      "Key Association: Exclusively breastfed infants are at high risk for Vitamin D deficiency because breast milk contains only trace amounts of the vitamin. The 'buzzy' finding of 'widening of the wrist' or 'splaying' at the growth plates is a hallmark of rickets on the USMLE."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D is typically low or normal in rickets. Even though PTH upregulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the kidney, the lack of the substrate (25-hydroxyvitamin D) prevents an increase in the active form.",
      "Option C: Serum calcium is typically low or at the low end of the normal range due to decreased intestinal absorption.",
      "Option D: Serum phosphate is typically low in rickets because Vitamin D deficiency reduces its absorption, and elevated PTH further increases phosphate excretion in the urine.",
      "Option E: Unconjugated bilirubin is unrelated to the pathophysiology of rickets or bone mineralization; it is typically elevated in hemolytic conditions or hepatic conjugation defects like Crigler-Najjar syndrome."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Nutritional rickets in exclusively breastfed infants presents with low calcium, low phosphate, high PTH, and high alkaline phosphatase activity.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Q #1351 \u2013 Pathology",
    "id": "e5a74caf"
