      "Primary Mechanism: Biliary atresia is a neonatal condition characterized by the progressive fibro-obliterative destruction of the extrahepatic biliary tree. This obstruction prevents the secretion of conjugated bile into the small intestine, leading to cholestasis and conjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Without surgical intervention (Kasai procedure), it leads to biliary cirrhosis and liver failure.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: This patient presents with pathologic jaundice based on two criteria: the jaundice is present after one week of life in a term infant, and the direct (conjugated) bilirubin is greater than 10% of the total bilirubin (10 mg/dL is ~77% of 13 mg/dL). The normal hemoglobin level (15 g/dL) effectively rules out hemolytic causes of jaundice.",
      "Key Association: Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia in a neonate is biliary atresia until proven otherwise. Distractors like 'difficulty attaching' are often included to lead students toward breastfeeding jaundice, but the presence of conjugated (direct) hyperbilirubinemia points away from that diagnosis."
