    "correct_answer": "D: Increased production of leukotrienes",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": {
      "system": "Biochemistry",
      "discipline": "Pharmacology"
    },
    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Ibuprofen is a non-selective inhibitor of cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes. These enzymes are responsible for converting arachidonic acid, a membrane phospholipid derivative, into prostaglandins, prostacyclins, and thromboxanes. When the COX pathway is pharmacologically blocked, the pool of available arachidonic acid is shunted toward the alternative lipoxygenase (LOX) pathway.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The LOX pathway converts arachidonic acid into leukotrienes (LTC4, LTD4, LTE4). An increase in these leukotrienes can lead to bronchoconstriction, which is the physiological basis for NSAID-exacerbated respiratory disease (NERD), also known as aspirin-induced asthma. While this patient is taking the medication for muscle pain, the biochemical shift toward leukotriene production occurs systemically.",
      "Key Association: The 'shunting' of arachidonic acid from the COX pathway to the LOX pathway is a high-yield biochemical concept for understanding NSAID side effects."
    ],
