      "Primary Mechanism: Tetracyclines, such as doxycycline, are antibiotics that inhibit the 30S ribosomal subunit. They are commonly used to treat atypical pneumonias caused by organisms like Mycoplasma pneumoniae, Legionella, and Chlamydia.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's presentation of blisters on sun-exposed areas (lips and cheeks) shortly after starting antibiotic therapy for pneumonia is characteristic of drug-induced photosensitivity. Doxycycline is a frequent cause of this phototoxic reaction. While macrolides (50S inhibitors) are also used for Mycoplasma, they do not typically cause photosensitivity.",
