    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: The patient's presentation of recurrent urge incontinence (detrusor hyperactivity) and upper motor neuron (UMN) signs (Babinski sign, clonus) in a young woman is highly suggestive of Multiple Sclerosis (MS). MS is an autoimmune inflammatory demyelinating disease of the central nervous system. A classic neuro-ophthalmologic finding in MS is Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia (INO), which results from a lesion in the Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus (MLF).",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The MLF is a highly myelinated tract that coordinates conjugate horizontal gaze by connecting the abducens nucleus (CN VI) on one side to the contralateral oculomotor nucleus (CN III). In INO, the eye ipsilateral to the MLF lesion cannot adduct during horizontal gaze. However, because the convergence reflex utilizes a different neural pathway that bypasses the MLF to activate the medial rectus muscles, convergence remains intact.",
      "Key Association: Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia (MLF syndrome) and optic neuritis are pathognomonic 'buzzword' associations for Multiple Sclerosis in young patients."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Incorrect because downward mobility of the vesicourethral junction is the classic mechanism for stress incontinence, typically seen in multiparous women due to pelvic floor laxity, not urge incontinence associated with MS.",
      "Option B: Incorrect because fasciculations are a lower motor neuron (LMN) sign. While Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) features both UMN and LMN signs, it does not typically present with the autonomic (bladder) or sensory symptoms characteristic of MS.",
      "Option C: Incorrect because INO involves an inability to adduct the eye on the side of the lesion during horizontal gaze. Abduction is controlled by the abducens nerve (CN VI) and is generally preserved, though the abducting eye often exhibits nystagmus."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Internuclear ophthalmoplegia, a hallmark of Multiple Sclerosis, is characterized by impaired adduction during horizontal gaze with preserved convergence, indicating an intact oculomotor nerve and medial rectus function via non-MLF pathways.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Q #1176 \u2013 Neuro",
