      "Primary Mechanism: DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion syndrome) is a microdeletion syndrome characterized by the developmental failure of the third and fourth pharyngeal pouches. This failure is fundamentally attributed to the defective migration and differentiation of neural crest cells into the pharyngeal apparatus during embryogenesis. These cells are critical for the formation of the thymus (3rd pouch), the inferior parathyroid glands (3rd pouch), the superior parathyroid glands (4th pouch), and the conotruncal region of the heart.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The presence of a holosystolic murmur (suggestive of a VSD) in a neonate with a cleft lip and palate strongly points toward DiGeorge syndrome. The 'CATCH-22' mnemonic summarizes the classic findings: Cardiac defects (conotruncal), Abnormal facies, Thymic hypoplasia (leading to T-cell deficiency), Cleft palate, and Hypocalcemia (due to parathyroid hypoplasia).",
      "Key Association: A high-yield distinction for exams is that DiGeorge syndrome affects the pharyngeal pouches (endoderm), whereas the underlying cause is the failure of neural crest cells to migrate into those areas."
