    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Nifedipine is a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker (CCB) that primarily acts on the smooth muscle of arterioles. By blocking L-type calcium channels, it induces significant arteriolar vasodilation, which decreases total peripheral resistance and afterload.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The peripheral edema associated with dihydropyridine CCBs (like nifedipine and amlodipine) is a common side effect. It occurs because the preferential dilation of precapillary arterioles increases capillary hydrostatic pressure, leading to fluid extravasation into the interstitial space. This is distinct from the edema caused by heart failure or renal disease.",
      "Key Association: When a patient presents with sudden fluid retention or peripheral edema shortly after starting an antihypertensive, always check for dihydropyridine CCBs on their med list."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Amiodarone is a Class III antiarrhythmic that blocks potassium channels. Its classic side effects include blue-gray skin discoloration, drug-induced thyroiditis (hypo- or hyperthyroidism), and pulmonary fibrosis, but not typically peripheral edema.",
      "Option B: Hydralazine is an arterial vasodilator used in hypertensive emergencies and pregnancy. While it can cause reflex tachycardia and fluid retention, its most high-yield side effect for exams is drug-induced lupus erythematosus, characterized by anti-histone antibodies.",
      "Option D: Verapamil is a non-dihydropyridine CCB that acts primarily on the calcium channels of the SA and AV nodes. Its most characteristic side effect is constipation, rather than peripheral edema."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers like nifedipine cause peripheral edema through preferential arteriolar vasodilation and increased capillary hydrostatic pressure.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Q #1156 \u2013 Pharm",
