      "Clinical Reasoning: The positive eosin-5-maleimide (EMA) binding test is a highly specific modern diagnostic tool for HS. The patient's increased MCHC is a classic laboratory finding, reflecting mild dehydration of the spherocytes. Furthermore, the father's history of splenectomy (to treat hemolysis) and cholecystectomy (likely due to recurrent calcium bilirubinate pigment stones from chronic hemolysis) strongly supports an inherited hemolytic anemia.",
