      "Clinical Reasoning: The classic presentation of AIP is often summarized by the '5 Ps': Painful abdomen, Port wine-colored urine, Polyneuropathy, Psychological disturbances, and Precipitated by drugs (like cytochrome P450 inducers, alcohol, or starvation). This patient exhibits psychiatric symptoms (depression/suicidal ideation), abdominal pain triggered by alcohol, and neurological dysfunction (positive Romberg sign indicating dorsal column involvement or peripheral neuropathy). The family history in a mother and brother supports an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern.",
