    "correct_answer": "E: Preserved ejection fraction",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": {
      "system": "Cardiology",
      "discipline": "Physiology"
    },
    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Aortic stenosis (AS) leads to increased afterload on the left ventricle. To overcome this resistance, the ventricle undergoes concentric hypertrophy, where sarcomeres are added in parallel. This thickening of the ventricular wall decreases compliance, leading to diastolic dysfunction. An S4 heart sound is a classic sign of blood hitting a stiff, non-compliant ventricular wall during atrial contraction.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: Diastolic heart failure (HFpEF) is characterized by a preserved ejection fraction (typically >50%). While the heart's ability to fill is impaired due to stiffness, its contractile function (pumping) remains relatively intact in the early to mid-stages of the disease.",
      "Key Association: S4 = Diastolic dysfunction = Preserved Ejection Fraction. S3 = Systolic dysfunction = Reduced Ejection Fraction."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Incorrect because diffuse four-chamber dilatation with reduced ejection fraction describes dilated cardiomyopathy, which is associated with an S3 heart sound and systolic dysfunction.",
      "Option B: Incorrect because rheumatic heart disease is the most common cause of mitral stenosis, which presents with an opening snap and a mid-to-late diastolic murmur.",
      "Option C: Incorrect because sarcomeres laid in series describe eccentric hypertrophy, which occurs in response to volume overload (e.g., aortic regurgitation) rather than the pressure overload seen in AS.",
      "Option D: Incorrect because PCWP (Pulmonary Capillary Wedge Pressure) reflects left atrial pressure. In AS, the pressure backs up into the left atrium, leading to an increased PCWP. Normal PCWP is seen in cor pulmonale (right heart failure due to pulmonary causes).",
      "Option F: Incorrect because pulsus paradoxus (a drop in systolic BP >10 mmHg during inspiration) is most commonly associated with cardiac tamponade, severe asthma, or COPD."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Aortic stenosis causes concentric hypertrophy and diastolic dysfunction, which is characterized by an S4 heart sound and a preserved ejection fraction.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Q #1160 \u2013 Cardio",
    "id": "5fa7fc04"
