    "question": "A 68-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of intermittent claudication in his buttocks and thighs. He has a history of coronary artery bypass grafting 4 years ago, a 50-pack-year smoking history, and hypertension. His current medications include a statin, an ACE inhibitor, and aspirin. Physical examination reveals loss of hair over the shins and reduced popliteal and dorsalis pedis pulses. There is no hyperpigmentation or peripheral edema. Jugular venous pressure (JVP) is 3 cm above the sternal angle. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in diagnosis?",
