    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Peripheral Arterial Disease (PAD) is primarily caused by atherosclerosis of the peripheral arteries, leading to narrowed lumens and reduced blood flow. During exercise, the increased oxygen demand of the muscles cannot be met, resulting in ischemic pain (claudication). The Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI) is the gold-standard initial screening tool, calculated by dividing the higher systolic blood pressure in the ankle by the higher systolic blood pressure in the arm. A ratio of <0.9 is diagnostic of PAD.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: This patient presents with classic risk factors (smoking, hypertension, known coronary artery disease) and physical findings (atrophic skin changes like hair loss, diminished distal pulses) consistent with PAD. The absence of hyperpigmentation and edema makes venous disease less likely, and a normal JVP (3 cm above the sternal angle is the upper limit of normal) suggests the absence of right heart failure.",
      "Key Association: The 'triad' of a statin, ACE inhibitor (or ARB), and an antiplatelet agent (aspirin) is the standard medical management for patients with established atherosclerotic disease, including PAD and carotid stenosis."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option B: Arteriography is the most definitive imaging modality but is invasive and typically reserved for patients in whom surgical or endovascular intervention is already planned, rather than as an initial diagnostic step.",
      "Option C: Duplex ultrasonography is highly useful for diagnosing venous insufficiency (indicated by edema or hyperpigmentation) or for localizing specific arterial stenoses after PAD has been confirmed by ABI.",
      "Option D: Echocardiography is used to evaluate cardiac structure and function (ejection fraction). While this patient has cardiovascular risk, his normal JVP and lack of pulmonary symptoms suggest heart failure is not the cause of his current presentation.",
      "Option E: Exercise stress testing may be used to assess exercise tolerance or to confirm the diagnosis in patients with borderline ABIs, but it is generally performed after an initial resting ABI."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: The initial diagnostic step for suspected peripheral arterial disease is the Ankle-Brachial Index (ABI), where a ratio <0.9 confirms the diagnosis.",
