    "question": "A 50-year-old male presents with a 1-hour history of severe abdominal pain. His medical history is significant for alcoholic cirrhosis and a previous episode of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis. On physical examination, Trousseau and Chvostek signs are positive. Laboratory studies show a serum alkaline phosphatase (ALP) level of 120 U/L (normal range: 50-150 U/L). Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
