    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: In renal failure, several electrolyte imbalances occur predictably. Hypocalcemia develops because the damaged kidneys cannot reabsorb calcium in the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and fail to activate 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT). Without active Vitamin D (1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D3), intestinal calcium absorption decreases. Hyperphosphatemia occurs because the kidneys can no longer effectively filter and excrete phosphate. Furthermore, the loss of PTH-mediated phosphate excretion in the PCT leads to increased reabsorption.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: Renal failure typically presents with a High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (HAGMA). Uremia (the 'U' in the MUDPILES mnemonic) represents the accumulation of organic acids that the kidney fails to excrete. To identify HAGMA, one must calculate the anion gap [Na - (Cl + HCO3)]. Assuming a normal sodium of 140, Option D yields an anion gap of 20 (140 - 120), which is elevated (>12). Option D also correctly reflects the expected low calcium, high phosphate, and low bicarbonate levels.",
      "Key Association: Remember 'MUDPILES' for high anion gap metabolic acidosis; Uremia is a classic cause. Also, recall that renal failure leads to secondary hyperparathyroidism due to the low calcium and high phosphate levels."
    ],
