    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) is a type II hypersensitivity reaction. It is characterized by the formation of IgG antibodies against the complex of heparin and platelet factor 4 (PF4). These antibodies bind to the heparin-PF4 complex on the surface of platelets, leading to platelet activation, consumption, and a paradoxical prothrombotic state.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's significant drop in platelet count (from a presumed normal baseline to 80,000/mm\u00b3) approximately one week after starting heparin is the classic presentation of HIT. Type II hypersensitivity reactions involve antibodies (IgG or IgM) directed against antigens located on specific cell surfaces or tissues. Graves disease is also a type II hypersensitivity reaction, where thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI) bind to and activate the TSH receptor on thyroid follicular cells.",
      "Key Association: Platelets are cells; therefore, an antibody-mediated attack against them (or complexes on their surface) is categorized as Type II hypersensitivity. Other high-yield Type II examples include pernicious anemia (anti-parietal cell antibodies), bullous pemphigoid (anti-hemidesmosome), and pemphigus vulgaris (anti-desmosome)."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Arthus reaction is a type III hypersensitivity reaction involving the formation and deposition of immune complexes. It typically presents as localized erythema and edema at an injection site 3 to 8 hours after exposure in a sensitized individual.",
      "Option C: Multiple sclerosis is primarily a type IV (cell-mediated) hypersensitivity reaction involving T-cell-mediated destruction of oligodendrocytes and myelin basic protein.",
      "Option D: The PPD (tuberculin) skin test is a classic example of a type IV (delayed-type) hypersensitivity reaction, which is mediated by T-lymphocytes rather than antibodies. Other examples include contact dermatitis (e.g., nickel, latex, poison ivy).",
      "Option E: Urticaria (hives) is a type I hypersensitivity reaction, which is IgE-mediated and involves the cross-linking of IgE on mast cells and basophils, leading to the release of histamine."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) is a type II hypersensitivity reaction involving IgG antibodies against the heparin-PF4 complex on platelets, sharing a mechanism with conditions like Graves disease and pernicious anemia.",
