    "question": "A 31-year-old woman with a history of multiple sclerosis (MS) presents with urgent incontinence. She reports needing to void 8 to 12 times per day, often triggered by sticking her key in the front door or stepping out of her car. Her post-void residual volume is 60 mL. Physical examination reveals upper motor neuron findings, including a positive Babinski sign and clonus. Neurological examination shows that upon lateral gaze, the abducting eye exhibits nystagmus while the contralateral eye fails to adduct; however, convergence is preserved. Which of the following is the most likely underlying pathophysiology of her condition?",
