      "Option B: Ethylene glycol (found in antifreeze) can cause HAGMA and is associated with carwash/automotive work, but it typically presents with renal failure and calcium oxalate crystals in the urine, not a surgical abdomen.",
      "Option C: Methanol toxicity (associated with home-brewed alcohol) causes HAGMA and can lead to blindness, but it does not explain the localized RLQ tenderness and leukocytosis.",
      "Option D: Salicylate toxicity (aspirin) typically causes a mixed acid-base disorder: a primary respiratory alkalosis followed by a primary metabolic acidosis. While the patient takes aspirin, the clinical picture of sepsis is more consistent with the findings."
