      "Primary Mechanism: Minimal Change Disease (MCD), also known as lipoid nephrosis, is the most common cause of nephrotic syndrome in children. The underlying pathophysiology involves the effacement of podocyte foot processes, which is visible only on electron microscopy. This leads to the loss of the glomerular polyanion (negative charge) barrier, resulting in selective proteinuria (primarily albuminuria).",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's presentation of edema and proteinuria without hematuria (no RBCs) is classic for nephrotic syndrome. In response to the resulting hypoalbuminemia and decreased plasma oncotic pressure, the liver increases the synthesis of various proteins, including apolipoproteins. This leads to hyperlipidemia, characterized by increased serum LDL and VLDL levels.",
