      "Primary Mechanism: Fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD) is a non-inflammatory, non-atherosclerotic vascular disease that leads to stenosis of medium-sized arteries, most commonly the renal and carotid arteries. In the renal arteries, this stenosis reduces renal perfusion pressure. The juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) senses this decrease and responds by secreting renin. Renin initiates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), leading to increased aldosterone production from the adrenal zona glomerulosa.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient presents with secondary hyperaldosteronism, evidenced by high renin, hypokalemia (3.3 mEq/L), and metabolic alkalosis (bicarbonate 29 mEq/L). While primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) also causes hypokalemia and alkalosis, it would result in suppressed (low) renin due to negative feedback from volume expansion. The high renin level indicates the problem is proximal to the adrenal gland, specifically reduced renal blood flow.",
      "Key Association: FMD is the classic cause of renovascular hypertension in younger patients (traditionally females 20-40, though it can occur in males). It is characterized by a 'string of beads' appearance on angiography due to alternating areas of stenosis and aneurysmal dilation."
