    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Incorrect because the 9;22 translocation (Philadelphia chromosome) results in the BCR-ABL fusion protein, which is the hallmark of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML). CML smears typically show a full spectrum of maturing myeloid cells (myelocytes, metamyelocytes) rather than smudge cells.",
      "Option B: Incorrect because the 15;17 translocation is associated with Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL, M3 subtype of AML). This condition is characterized by the presence of Auer rods and a high risk of disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC).",
      "Option D: Incorrect because CD10 and TDT (Terminal Deoxynucleotidyl Transferase) are markers for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL), which is the most common leukemia in children and presents with lymphoblasts on the smear."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) typically presents in elderly patients with smudge cells on peripheral smear and is characterized by the co-expression of CD5 and CD23 on B cells.",
