      "Primary Mechanism: This patient is presenting with gallstone pancreatitis, a complication of choledocholithiasis. When a gallstone migrates from the gallbladder into the common bile duct and becomes lodged distally at the hepatopancreatic ampulla (Ampulla of Vater), it obstructs both the biliary tree and the pancreatic duct. This leads to a retrograde backup of bile (causing obstructive jaundice) and pancreatic enzymes (causing autodigestion of the pancreas and elevated amylase/lipase).",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The elevated total bilirubin and ALP indicate an obstructive (cholestatic) pattern of jaundice. The concurrent elevation in amylase specifically points to pancreatic involvement. The negative Sudan black stain indicates there is no steatorrhea, which helps rule out chronic pancreatic insufficiency.",
