      "Primary Mechanism: Rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is a systemic autoimmune disease that can involve various extra-articular structures, including the heart and lungs. Pericarditis is a known complication of RA. The classic presentation of pericarditis involves pleuritic chest pain that is relieved by leaning forward (positional relief), which explains why the patient is 'bent over at the waist.'",
      "Clinical Reasoning: On an electrocardiogram (ECG), acute pericarditis typically manifests as diffuse ST-segment elevations across most leads, often accompanied by PR-segment depression. This differs from a myocardial infarction, where ST elevations are localized to specific contiguous leads corresponding to a vascular territory.",
