    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": {
      "system": "Hematology/Oncology",
      "discipline": "Pathology"
    },
    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Burkitt lymphoma is characterized by the t(8;14) translocation, which moves the c-MYC proto-oncogene from chromosome 8 to the immunoglobulin heavy chain (IgH) locus on chromosome 14. This results in the constitutive overexpression of the c-MYC protein. c-MYC is a transcription factor that plays a central role in cell cycle progression, apoptosis, and cellular transformation by regulating the expression of numerous genes involved in growth and metabolism.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The 'starry sky' appearance is the classic histological hallmark of Burkitt lymphoma. The 'stars' are tingible body macrophages containing phagocytosed apoptotic debris, while the 'sky' is composed of rapidly proliferating, basophilic B cells. This high rate of turnover and proliferation is directly driven by the dysregulated c-MYC transcription factor.",
      "Key Association: The podcast emphasizes the distinction between Burkitt lymphoma (t(8;14), c-MYC, transcription factor) and Follicular lymphoma (t(14;18), BCL-2, anti-apoptotic molecule)."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Incorrect because fusion proteins with tyrosine kinase activity, such as BCR-ABL, are characteristic of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) via the t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome.",
      "Option B: Incorrect because BCL-2 is an anti-apoptotic molecule associated with Follicular lymphoma, typically involving a t(14;18) translocation.",
      "Option D: Incorrect because while TP53 (mutated in Li-Fraumeni syndrome) regulates the cell cycle to allow for DNA repair, it is not the primary driver in the t(8;14) translocation of Burkitt lymphoma."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Burkitt lymphoma is driven by a t(8;14) translocation that results in the overexpression of the c-MYC transcription factor, leading to a high-grade B-cell neoplasm with a characteristic 'starry sky' histological appearance.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Q #1061 \u2013 Pathology",
    "id": "dfe13cbc"
