    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Chronic bladder outlet obstruction, such as that caused by benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH), leads to progressive urinary retention. While the detrusor muscle may initially undergo compensatory hypertrophy, chronic overdistension eventually leads to 'detrusor burnout' or a hypocontractile bladder state.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The presence of a suprapubic mass (distended bladder) and elevated creatinine (post-renal azotemia) confirms significant obstruction. In this state, the bladder becomes a passive reservoir that fills until the intravesical pressure exceeds the urethral resistance, resulting in the involuntary dribbling of urine known as overflow incontinence.",
      "Key Association: BPH is a classic cause of overflow incontinence due to the mechanical obstruction of the urethra and subsequent failure of the detrusor muscle."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Detrusor hyperactivity is the underlying mechanism for urge incontinence (overactive bladder), which is characterized by a sudden, intense urge to void. It is commonly associated with UTIs, multiple sclerosis, or idiopathic causes, rather than chronic obstruction.",
      "Option B: A fractional excretion of sodium (FeNa) < 1% is characteristic of pre-renal azotemia (e.g., dehydration or heart failure), where the kidneys aggressively reabsorb sodium. In post-renal azotemia, the FeNa is typically > 1% due to tubular damage from back-pressure.",
      "Option C: Renal insufficiency typically leads to a metabolic acidosis, which is characterized by a decreased serum bicarbonate level, not an increase.",
      "Option D: Renal failure and uremia are causes of a High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (HAGMA), part of the 'MUDPILES' mnemonic. A normal anion gap acidosis is more typical of renal tubular acidosis or diarrhea."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) causes post-renal azotemia and overflow incontinence through chronic bladder outlet obstruction and subsequent detrusor muscle failure.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Q #1058 \u2013 Genitourinary",
