    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Listeria monocytogenes is a facultative intracellular, gram-positive rod. It is one of the three most common causes of neonatal sepsis, meningitis, and pneumonia, alongside Group B Streptococcus (GBS) and Escherichia coli. Listeria is typically acquired by the mother through the consumption of contaminated foods (e.g., deli meats, unpasteurized soft cheeses) and can be transmitted to the fetus transplacentally or during delivery.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The neonate's presentation of irritability and fever is highly suggestive of neonatal sepsis. While GBS and E. coli are more common, the specific laboratory finding of gram-positive rods is the definitive clue. GBS is a gram-positive coccus, and E. coli is a gram-negative rod, leaving Listeria as the only viable option among the common neonatal pathogens that fits the gram stain description.",
      "Key Association: In neonatal sepsis vignettes, always look for the Gram stain results: Gram-positive cocci point to GBS, Gram-negative rods point to E. coli, and Gram-positive rods point to Listeria."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Incorrect because Escherichia coli is a gram-negative rod, which contradicts the laboratory finding of gram-positive rods.",
      "Option B: Incorrect because Klebsiella pneumoniae is a gram-negative rod and is a less frequent cause of early-onset neonatal sepsis compared to the 'big three' (GBS, E. coli, Listeria).",
      "Option D: Incorrect because Streptococcus agalactiae (GBS) is a gram-positive coccus. While it is the most common cause of neonatal sepsis, it does not match the rod morphology described in the lab report."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Differentiate the common causes of neonatal sepsis (GBS, E. coli, and Listeria) by their Gram stain morphology: GBS is a gram-positive coccus, E. coli is a gram-negative rod, and Listeria is a gram-positive rod.",
