      "Primary Mechanism: The patient is presenting with symptoms of diabetic gastroparesis, a complication of long-standing, poorly controlled diabetes mellitus (indicated by an HbA1c of 12%). Gastroparesis results from autonomic neuropathy of the vagus nerve, leading to delayed gastric emptying. This can manifest as postprandial fullness, nausea, vomiting, or, as seen here, symptoms mimicking GERD (burning chest pain).",
      "Clinical Reasoning: While the clinical picture strongly suggests gastroparesis (supported by concurrent peripheral neuropathy and dysautonomia/orthostatic hypotension), the standard of care requires ruling out a mechanical or physical obstruction (such as a tumor or stricture) before confirming a functional motility disorder. Therefore, an upper endoscopy (EGD) is the necessary initial step.",
