      "Primary Mechanism: von Willebrand factor (vWF) is a crucial multimeric protein that facilitates platelet adhesion to the vascular subendothelium by bridging the platelet glycoprotein Ib (GpIb) receptor to exposed collagen. It also serves as a carrier protein for Factor VIII, protecting it from premature degradation in the plasma. von Willebrand Disease (vWD), typically inherited in an autosomal dominant fashion, results in a deficiency or dysfunction of vWF, leading to impaired primary hemostasis.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's presentation of mucosal bleeding (menorrhagia and epistaxis) is characteristic of a primary hemostatic defect. The laboratory profile\u2014normal platelet count, normal PT, and normal PTT, but an isolated increase in bleeding time\u2014is highly suggestive of vWD. Although vWF stabilizes Factor VIII, the PTT is only prolonged in approximately 50% of cases; therefore, a normal PTT does not exclude the diagnosis.",
      "Key Association: von Willebrand factor bridges Gp1b on the platelet to underlying collagen/vascular endothelium."
