      "Primary Mechanism: Graves' disease is a classic example of a Type II hypersensitivity reaction. In this condition, thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI) are produced, which act as autoantibodies that bind to and activate the TSH receptor on thyroid follicular cells, leading to autonomous overproduction of thyroid hormones. Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) is also a Type II hypersensitivity reaction. In HIT, IgG antibodies form against the complex of heparin and platelet factor 4 (PF4) on the surface of platelets, leading to platelet activation and consumption.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's symptoms of palpitations, heat intolerance, and exophthalmos, combined with sinus tachycardia and suppressed TSH, are diagnostic of Graves' disease. Identifying the underlying hypersensitivity type (Type II) allows for the selection of HIT, which also involves antibodies directed against cell-surface components.",
      "Key Association: Remember that Type II hypersensitivity involves antibodies (IgG or IgM) binding to antigens on specific cells or tissues, whereas Type III involves soluble immune complexes."
