      "Primary Mechanism: Reactive granulocytosis, also known as a leukemoid reaction, is an exaggerated white blood cell response to an underlying stimulus, most commonly a severe infection, inflammation, or physical stress. It is defined by a leukocyte count typically exceeding 30,000-50,000/mm\u00b3. The bone marrow releases a large number of mature and immature neutrophils (left shift) into the circulation. A key diagnostic feature to distinguish this from Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is the Leukocyte Alkaline Phosphatase (LAP) score, which is elevated in a leukemoid reaction but decreased in CML.",
