    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Streptococcus viridans (including species like S. mutans, S. mitis, and S. sanguinis) are alpha-hemolytic organisms. Alpha-hemolysis refers to the partial oxidation of hemoglobin, which results in a characteristic green discoloration (green zone) around colonies grown on blood agar.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The clinical presentation of subacute endocarditis (fever, new murmur) following a dental procedure (root canal) strongly points toward S. viridans. These organisms are normal oropharyngeal flora that can enter the bloodstream during dental work. They produce dextrans (carbohydrates) from glucose, which allows them to adhere to fibrin-platelet aggregates on previously damaged heart valves (e.g., mitral valve prolapse or prior regurgitation).",
      "Key Association: S. viridans is alpha-hemolytic (green on blood agar) and is differentiated from Streptococcus pneumoniae by being optochin-resistant and bile-insoluble."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Coagulase positivity is the hallmark of Staphylococcus aureus. While S. aureus is a common cause of endocarditis, it typically presents as an acute infection with rapid valve destruction, often in the setting of IV drug use, rather than a subacute presentation following dental work.",
      "Option C: Optochin sensitivity is used to identify Streptococcus pneumoniae. While S. pneumoniae is also alpha-hemolytic, it is not a common cause of subacute endocarditis following dental procedures.",
      "Option D: Small curvy growth on Campy agar (Skirrow medium) at 42\u00b0C is characteristic of Campylobacter jejuni, which causes inflammatory diarrhea and is associated with Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome.",
      "Option E: Tumbling motility at room temperature is a classic diagnostic feature of Listeria monocytogenes, a gram-positive rod that causes neonatal meningitis and sepsis in immunocompromised individuals."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Streptococcus viridans is an alpha-hemolytic (green zone on blood agar), optochin-resistant organism that is a leading cause of subacute bacterial endocarditis following dental procedures.",
