    "question": "A 45-year-old male with a history of chronic alcohol consumption presents for a routine follow-up. Physical examination reveals mild hepatomegaly. Laboratory studies show elevated aspartate aminotransferase (AST) and alanine aminotransferase (ALT) levels, with an AST:ALT ratio of 2:1. A liver biopsy is performed, and histopathological examination reveals hepatocytes with large, clear intracytoplasmic vacuoles that displace the nuclei to the periphery. Which of the following is the most likely cause of these findings?",
