      "Option C: Incorrect because gallstone pancreatitis would present with significantly elevated amylase and lipase levels, and requires a gallbladder to source the obstructing stone.",
      "Option E: Incorrect because Primary Sclerosing Cholangitis (PSC) typically presents with Charcot's triad (fever, jaundice, and RUQ pain) and is often associated with inflammatory bowel disease; this patient is afebrile and lacks the classic triad."
