      "Primary Mechanism: The presence of apple-green birefringence on Congo red stain indicates the presence of amyloid, which is a classic histological finding in medullary thyroid cancer (MTC). MTC arises from parafollicular C-cells and is frequently associated with Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN) syndromes, specifically MEN 2A and 2B. Both of these syndromes involve a high risk of pheochromocytoma.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: Before any surgical intervention for MTC (like a thyroidectomy), it is critical to screen for a co-existing pheochromocytoma. If a patient has an undiagnosed pheochromocytoma, the stress of surgery or anesthesia can trigger a massive release of catecholamines, leading to a life-threatening hypertensive crisis (paroxysmal palpitations, headache, and surge in blood pressure).",
