      "Clinical Reasoning: In a young patient with a history of 'heavy partying,' cocaine use is a high-yield trigger for serous pericarditis. Other common causes include viral infections (Coxsackievirus), autoimmune diseases (SLE), or uremia. The most characteristic finding on an electrocardiogram (ECG) for pericarditis is diffuse, concave ST-segment elevation across most leads, often accompanied by PR-segment depression.",
