      "Option B: Increased pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) indicates left-sided heart failure or cardiogenic shock. While pericarditis can lead to tamponade, the primary diagnosis here is suggested by the positional relief of pain.",
      "Option C: Increased serum creatine kinase (CK) can be seen in musculoskeletal conditions like pleurodynia (Bornholm disease), which causes sharp lateral chest pain due to intercostal muscle spasms, but it does not typically present with positional relief.",
      "Option D: Pulsus paradoxus (a drop in systolic BP >10 mmHg during inspiration) is a hallmark of cardiac tamponade, severe asthma, or COPD. While pericarditis can progress to tamponade, it is not the most likely initial finding for simple acute pericarditis."
