      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's presentation of brawny edema and hyperpigmentation, combined with normal peripheral pulses (3+), strongly suggests a venous rather than an arterial etiology. While the patient has risk factors for peripheral arterial disease (smoking, diabetes), the physical exam findings of brisk pulses and specific skin changes point toward CVI. Duplex ultrasonography is the gold standard for visualizing venous reflux and confirming the diagnosis.",
