      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's age (59), non-smoking status, and 6-month history of progressive dyspnea and dry cough are classic for IPF. The physical exam finding of diffuse dry inspiratory crackles (often called 'Velcro' crackles) is a hallmark of interstitial lung disease. The loud P2 indicates pulmonary hypertension, which in this case is Group 3 (due to lung disease). The normal PCWP is crucial as it rules out left heart pathology (Group 2) as the cause of the pulmonary hypertension or the shortness of breath.",
      "Key Association: A 'reticulonodular pattern' on chest X-ray is the classic radiographic description for the interstitial changes and honeycombing seen in pulmonary fibrosis."
