      "Primary Mechanism: Wilson disease is caused by a mutation in the ATP7B gene on chromosome 13, which encodes a copper-transporting ATPase. This defect leads to an inability to secrete copper into the bile and a failure to incorporate copper into ceruloplasmin. Consequently, copper accumulates in various tissues, most notably the liver and the basal ganglia.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: When copper deposits in the basal ganglia, specifically the putamen, it can manifest as Parkinsonism. This includes symptoms such as shuffling gait, cogwheel rigidity, pill-rolling tremors, and micrographia. In a young patient, the presence of Parkinsonian symptoms should immediately raise suspicion for Wilson disease.",
      "Key Association: The podcast emphasizes that 'Parkinsonism in a young patient is Wilson disease until proven otherwise.' The specific site of brain deposition to remember for the USMLE is the putamen."
