    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": {
      "system": "Endocrine",
      "discipline": "Physiology"
    },
    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Sarcoidosis is characterized by the presence of non-caseating granulomas, which are composed of epithelioid cells (activated macrophages). These macrophages uniquely express 1-alpha-hydroxylase, the enzyme responsible for converting inactive 25-hydroxyvitamin D into its active form, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol).",
      "Clinical Reasoning: Elevated calcitriol levels significantly increase the absorption of calcium from the small intestine into the bloodstream. This increased absorption leads to a decrease in fecal calcium. The resulting hypercalcemia exerts negative feedback on the calcium-sensing receptors of the parathyroid glands, leading to suppressed serum PTH levels.",
      "Key Association: Granulomatous diseases like sarcoidosis or tuberculosis can cause hypercalcemia through extra-renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Crohn disease typically involves the terminal ileum, leading to bile salt malabsorption and subsequent fat malabsorption (steatorrhea). Excess fat in the intestinal lumen chelates calcium (saponification), which increases fecal calcium. Additionally, vitamin D malabsorption can lead to secondary hyperparathyroidism (elevated PTH).",
      "Option B: In hypoparathyroidism, low PTH levels result in decreased 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity in the kidneys. This leads to lower calcitriol levels and impaired intestinal calcium absorption, which would increase fecal calcium.",
      "Option C: Metastatic malignancy often causes hypercalcemia through local bone destruction or PTHrP secretion. While hypercalcemia suppresses PTH, the low calcitriol levels (due to low PTH) would result in decreased intestinal calcium absorption and increased fecal calcium.",
      "Option D: Liver disease (e.g., cirrhosis) impairs the initial 25-hydroxylation of vitamin D. This deficiency leads to low calcitriol, decreased intestinal calcium absorption (high fecal calcium), and potentially secondary hyperparathyroidism (high PTH) due to hypocalcemia."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Sarcoidosis causes suppressed PTH and low fecal calcium due to autonomous 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity in granulomatous macrophages, which increases intestinal calcium absorption.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Q #836 \u2013 Endocrine",
    "id": "460c0056"
