    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": {
      "system": "Gastrointestinal",
      "discipline": "Pathophysiology"
    },
    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Long-standing diabetes mellitus (typically >20 years) often leads to autonomic neuropathy, which can significantly impact the enteric nervous system. The gastrointestinal manifestations are due to a motility disorder. Specifically, damage to the hypogastric nerves (sympathetic fibers that are 'anti-peristaltic') leads to unopposed parasympathetic activity from the pelvic splanchnic nerves, resulting in diarrhea. Conversely, damage to the pelvic splanchnic nerves (pro-peristaltic) would result in severe constipation.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The patient's 30-year history of Type 1 Diabetes is the primary driver for multi-system neuropathy. Her orthostatic hypotension further confirms autonomic dysfunction, as the body fails to properly constrict blood vessels upon standing. In the GI tract, this neuropathy can manifest as gastroparesis, diarrhea, or constipation depending on which nerve fibers are most affected.",
      "Key Association: In the context of long-term diabetes, think of 'Motility Disorder' for GI symptoms and 'Autonomic Neuropathy' for orthostatic changes."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Exudative diarrhea is characterized by the presence of blood and/or mucus in the stool, typically seen in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) or invasive infections (e.g., Shigella, Salmonella, Campylobacter).",
      "Option B: Generalized malabsorption is characteristic of conditions like Celiac disease, which involves flattening of the intestinal villi and often presents with iron deficiency anemia or an abnormal D-xylose test.",
      "Option D: Osmotic diarrhea occurs when non-absorbable solutes (like lactose in lactase deficiency) remain in the gut lumen and pull water in via osmosis.",
      "Option E: Secretory diarrhea involves the active secretion of electrolytes into the lumen, often mediated by toxins (e.g., C. perfringens, ETEC) that increase cAMP or cGMP levels."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Chronic diabetes mellitus can cause autonomic neuropathy leading to GI motility disorders, where damage to sympathetic nerves results in diarrhea and damage to parasympathetic nerves results in constipation.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Q #811 \u2013 Gastro",
