      "Primary Mechanism: Hypercalcemia of malignancy is a common cause of elevated serum calcium with suppressed PTH. This can occur via three main mechanisms: secretion of Parathyroid Hormone-related Protein (PTHrP) by tumors (e.g., squamous cell carcinoma of the lung, renal cell carcinoma), local osteolytic bone destruction by metastases (e.g., breast cancer, multiple myeloma), or 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D production (e.g., lymphomas). In the case of metastatic breast cancer, cytokines such as IL-1, IL-6, and TNF are released, stimulating osteoclast activity and bone resorption.",
