      "Primary Mechanism: Bacteroides fragilis and Escherichia coli are the two most common normal flora within the gastrointestinal tract. Both are gram-negative rods. However, Bacteroides species are obligate anaerobes, meaning they cannot grow in the presence of oxygen.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: In this case, the Gram stain identified gram-negative rods, but the aerobic culture on MacConkey agar only yielded Enterococci (gram-positive cocci). If E. coli were the gram-negative rod present, it would have grown on the aerobic MacConkey agar and appeared as pink colonies (due to lactose fermentation). The absence of gram-negative growth on an aerobic culture despite being seen on Gram stain strongly suggests a strict anaerobe like Bacteroides.",
