      "Primary Mechanism: This patient presents with Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia (HHT), also known as Osler-Weber-Rendu syndrome. This is an autosomal dominant disorder characterized by abnormal angiogenesis, leading to the formation of telangiectasias (small dilated blood vessels) on the skin and mucous membranes, as well as arteriovenous malformations (AVMs) in internal organs.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: The triad of recurrent epistaxis (nosebleeds) since childhood, cutaneous telangiectasias (the red dots on fingers and tongue), and iron deficiency anemia (manifesting as fatigue due to chronic GI bleeding) is classic for HHT. The fatigue in this patient is likely due to occult gastrointestinal bleeding from telangiectasias within the gut, a common complication of the disorder.",
      "Key Association: High-yield associations for HHT include pulmonary AVMs, which can lead to right-to-left shunting, paradoxical emboli, or high-output heart failure. The condition is often linked to mutations in genes involved in the TGF-beta signaling pathway (e.g., ENG or ACVRL1)."
