      "Option C: A DNA virus like HSV-1 or HSV-2 can cause herpetic whitlow, which presents as painful, vesicular lesions on the finger, often seen in healthcare workers like dental hygienists. However, it does not cause chronic epistaxis or widespread telangiectasias.",
      "Option D: Raynaud phenomenon involves episodic vasospasm of the digital arteries, typically presenting with a triphasic color change (white to blue to red) in response to cold or stress, rather than persistent telangiectatic spots.",
