    "correct_answer": "D: Serum protein electrophoresis",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "tags": {
      "system": "Hematology",
      "discipline": "Pathology"
    },
    "correct_explanation": [
      "Primary Mechanism: Multiple Myeloma (MM) is a plasma cell dyscrasia characterized by the monoclonal proliferation of plasma cells. These malignant cells produce a monoclonal (M) protein, typically IgG or IgA. The initial screening step to identify this monoclonal gammopathy is Serum Protein Electrophoresis (SPEP), which reveals a characteristic 'M-spike'.",
      "Clinical Reasoning: This patient presents with the classic 'CRAB' criteria for Multiple Myeloma: Hypercalcemia (10.4 mg/dL), Renal insufficiency (Creatinine 4.6 mg/dL), Anemia (implied by fatigue), and Bone pain (central back pain). The nosebleeds are a high-yield finding representing uremic platelet dysfunction; the significantly elevated BUN (70 mg/dL) causes a qualitative platelet defect (normal count, increased bleeding time). The peripheral smear showing plasma cells with 'clock-face chromatin' confirms the underlying pathology.",
      "Key Association: While a bone marrow biopsy is the definitive/confirmatory test (requiring >10% plasma cells), SPEP is the next best step in the diagnostic workup following clinical suspicion."
    ],
    "incorrect_explanations": [
      "Option A: Bone marrow biopsy is the definitive diagnostic tool for Multiple Myeloma, but it is typically performed after screening with SPEP/UPEP has identified a monoclonal spike.",
      "Option B: Hemoglobin electrophoresis is used to diagnose hemoglobinopathies such as Sickle Cell Disease or Thalassemia, not plasma cell disorders.",
      "Option C: Karyotype analysis is used to identify chromosomal translocations, such as the t(9;22) Philadelphia chromosome seen in Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML).",
      "Option E: Thin and thick blood smears are the gold standard for diagnosing Malaria by visualizing parasites within red blood cells."
    ],
    "learning_objective": "The Bottom Line: Multiple Myeloma presents with CRAB symptoms (Calcium, Renal, Anemia, Bone); the next best step in diagnosis is Serum Protein Electrophoresis (SPEP) to identify an M-protein spike.",
    "source_episode": "HY USMLE Hematology Q - #319",
    "id": "7840654f"
