      "Option B: IgA nephropathy presents as synpharyngitic hematuria, occurring within 1-2 days of an upper respiratory or gastrointestinal infection, rather than as a delayed hypersensitivity to medication.",
      "Option D: Membranous glomerulonephritis is a nephrotic syndrome (presenting with heavy proteinuria and edema) and is associated with a 'spike and dome' appearance on biopsy. It is not an acute reaction to antibiotics.",
      "Option E: Renal papillary necrosis is associated with sickle cell disease, chronic NSAID use, or diabetes. It presents with gross hematuria and sloughed papillae in the urine, but lacks the systemic hypersensitivity signs like fever and rash."
